Northpeak Search’s higher‑than‑expected Dedicated Burst Usage charge was caused by a metering bug that double‑counted burst usage whenever a request was retried without a propagated idempotency_key; the retry attempts generated separate metering events, inflating GPU‑second totals  [1] [1].

The bug has been fixed by updating the billing pipeline to:
- Propagate the idempotency_key from the gateway to the metering event, and when it is missing, derive a stable hash from upstream trace/correlation IDs  [1];
- Enhance the de‑duplication logic to use this key (or the stable hash) so that retries do not create additional burst usage entries  [1];
- Add a reconciliation check that flags spikes from duplicate‑attempt events  [1].

These changes prevent future double‑counting of burst GPU‑seconds on retry attempts.
